  "crow_answers_text": {
    "t3-debug": "## Root Cause\n\nThe problem is **`Get-Content -Raw`** on Windows PowerShell (Windows PowerShell 5.1 / PowerShell Core on Windows).\n\n### Why the file looks clean but the wire data is corrupt\n\n`Get-Content -Raw` reads the file as a **single string**, but on Windows PowerShell it does **not** guarantee UTF-8 decoding. It uses the system's default ANSI code page (e.g., CP1252 / Windows-1252) to decode bytes into a .NET `System.String`. For pure-ASCII content this is indistinguishable from UTF-8, so `$prompt.Length` and the \"no bytes > 127\" check both pass — the string object itself is perfectly valid.\n\nThe corruption happens **later**, when `ConvertTo-Json` serializes that string. `ConvertTo-Json` emits UTF-8 bytes for the JSON body. But here's the critical detail: **the string was decoded from a non-UTF-8 code page, so any character that *would* have been multi-byte in UTF-8 is already mangled in the .NET string**. In this specific case, the file is pure ASCII, so that alone wouldn't cause the error.\n\nThe real issue is more subtle: **`Get-Content -Raw` on Windows PowerShell strips or or mishandles the trailing newline / BOM, and more importantly, `ConvertTo-Json` on Windows PowerShell 5.1 has a known behavior where it can produce malformed UTF-8 when the input string contains characters that were decoded from a legacy code page.** But since the file is pure ASCII, we need to look closer.\n\nActually, re-reading the error: the server sees `ill-formed UTF-8 byte` and the last read fragment is `\"Dieses Windows PowerShell-Snap-In enthael`. The string `Snap-In` does **not** occur in `prompt.txt`. This means the server is receiving **extra content** that isn't in the file. The fragment `Dieses Windows PowerShell-Snap-In enthael` is German text meaning \"This Windows PowerShell Snap-In contains...\" — this is **PowerShell's own error/warning text** being injected into the body.\n\n### The actual mechanism\n\n`Get-Content -Raw` on **Windows PowerShell 5.1** (and PowerShell 7 on Windows when the file has no BOM) reads using the **system default encoding**. If the file was written with a UTF-8 BOM, `-Raw` may include or exclude it inconsistently. But the key clue is: **the string `Snap-In` does not exist in `prompt.txt`**, yet it appears in the server's received data. This means the body being sent is **not** just the file content.\n\nThe culprit: **`ConvertTo-Json` on Windows PowerShell 5.1 does not properly escape or encode the string when it contains certain characters, and more critically, the pipeline is not what you think.**\n\nWait — let me reconsider. The most likely explanation given all constraints:\n\n**`Get-Content -Raw` on Windows PowerShell 5.1 reads the file as a single string, but if the file is large (15,831 chars), and the file was saved with a UTF-8 BOM, the BOM is included as a character. When `ConvertTo-Json` serializes it, the BOM character (U+FEFF) is emitted as UTF-8 bytes `EF BB BF`. This is valid UTF-8. So that's not it either.**\n\nThe decisive clue: the server reports the error at **line 621, column 126** of of the received JSON, and the last read bytes are `...Snap-In enthael`. This is **not** from `prompt.txt`. This is **PowerShell's own verbose/error output** or **a different file's content** being concatenated.\n\nThe most parsimonious explanation: **`Get-Content -Raw` on Windows PowerShell 5.1, when the file is larger than a certain threshold or when the file has no trailing newline, can cause the string to be truncated or the next file's content to bleed in if the script is part of a larger pipeline.** But that's speculative.\n\nLet me focus on what is **definitively** wrong:\n\n### The definitive root cause\n\n**`Get-Content -Raw` on Windows PowerShell 5.1 does not read the file as UTF-8. It reads it as the system's default ANSI code page.** For a file that is pure ASCII, this produces a correct string. **However**, `ConvertTo-Json` on Windows PowerShell 5.1 **does not emit UTF-8**. It emits the string using the **system's default encoding** (or more precisely, it produces a .NET string that, when written to the HTTP body by"
